{
  "title": "Ever\u00ae Demand\u2122 \u2014 marketplace (1,839\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Ever\u00ae Demand\u2122\nDomain: marketplace | Scale: large | Stars: 1,839\nProblem: Provides an open-source, real-time commerce platform for on-demand, collaborative, and sharing economies supporting single and multi-vendor marketplaces. It enables businesses to manage ordering, delivery logistics, inventory, and storefronts through a headless commerce framework.\nTarget users: Marketplace operators and platform owners, Merchants and store vendors, Delivery carriers and drivers, End customers placing on-demand orders, Platform administrators\nCore features: Multi-store / multi-vendor marketplace support; Real-time order management and processing; Headless commerce APIs (GraphQL, REST, WebSocket); Mobile apps for customers, carriers, and merchants (Ionic/React Native/Flutter); Delivery and shipping management with real-time location tracking; Payment gateway integration (Stripe); Inventory and stock management; Multi-language and internationalization (i18N) support\nIntegrations: Stripe (payment gateway), Socket.io (WebSocket real-time communication), Expo (React Native mobile), Flutter/Dart (mobile)\nTech stack: Angular 9 (Admin & Shopping Website), Ionic 5 (Mobile Apps), React Native/Expo (WIP), Flutter/Dart (WIP) + Node.js with NestJS (TypeScript) + MongoDB (inferred from NestJS/reactive stack) + Docker, Kubernetes, Lerna monorepo\nKey libraries: NestJS, RxJS, InversifyJS, Ionic, Angular, Socket.io, GraphQL, React Native, Expo, Lerna\nArchitecture: microservices\nDesign lessons:\n- Monorepo managed with Lerna and Yarn workspaces separates concerns across multiple packages (core API, admin, merchant, shop, carrier) while sharing common libraries.\n- Headless commerce design exposes GraphQL, REST, and WebSocket APIs, decoupling backend logic from multiple frontend implementations across web and mobile.\n- Heavy use of RxJS and reactive patterns throughout the platform ensures real-time data propagation for orders, inventory, and delivery tracking.\n- InversifyJS is used for IoC/DI on the frontend while NestJS handles DI on the backend, maintaining consistent inversion-of-control patterns across the full stack.",
